css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-d=
ecoration:underline">disposable agents</a><span>=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Ccutou=
ts,=E2=80=9D my colleague Neil MacFarquhar writes. They are often low-level=
 criminals, sometimes teenagers, who don=E2=80=99t know whom they=E2=80=99r=
e working for and agree to take on a task for a fee paid in cryptocurrency.=
</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margi=
n:0 0 15px"><span>Since the U.S. began its war in Iran in February, there h=
ave been about 20 small-scale arson and bombing attacks against Jewish and =
American targets in Europe and North America. The authorities and analysts =
said the incidents all followed a similar model: They were carried out by l=
ocal criminals or disenfranchised young men hired through anonymous online =
transactions, with a front organization for Iran=E2=80=99s Revolutionary Gu=
ards ultimately claiming responsibility.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal =
17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The plots don=E2=
=80=99t have to involve real danger to be disruptive. In the spring, after =
a warning that the Israeli Embassy in London would be attacked, two jars of=
 a powdered substance were found nearby, setting off a huge police response=
=2E The substance turned out to be harmless.</span></p><p style=3D"font:nor=
mal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Europeans hav=
e been divided about how they should treat these episodes. As terror incide=
nts or crimes=3F Hiring locals to do this work has the added effect of aggr=
avating local divisions, experts told Neil =E2=80=94 turning a story about =
Iranian proxies attacking a synagogue, for example, into one about an appar=
ent surge of antisemitism in Britain.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But they rarely invo=
lve hardened targets, like an air base.</span></p><table border=3D"0" cellp=
