zing he=E2=80=99d gotten in over his head and looking for a way out=3F</spa=
n></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0=
 15px"><span>But if this is an instance of gig economy warfare, experts tol=
d Neil, it represents an escalation in these efforts to use disaffected you=
ng people as agents of far-off governments.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>States like Ir=
an and Russia do this for multiple reasons. Partly because it ensures dista=
nce between the people carrying out the crimes and the state itself, one ex=
pert told Neil.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;c=
olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But another reason is that it=E2=80=99s ch=
eap. The term =E2=80=9Cgig economy warfare=E2=80=9D is compelling in part b=
ecause of the parallels between these actors and, say, underpaid delivery d=
